"text": "This is Best Visual Hooks, Episode 2, Clip Stitch. A clip stitch is when you use an already proven viral hook at the beginning of your video and then react to or transition from it. You've probably seen these crush for clothing brands, car dealerships, and especially Tony, this LCD sign guy. Now the reason these work so well is because millions of people have already validated that they reacted well to that visual hook. So you're just reusing what's already worked. If you do want to try these, there's a site called transitionalhooks.com that has a database of all of these clips that you can stitch in your hooks. But here's the catch with these. The clip stitch viral hook is a top-of-funnel math game. It drives insane reach, but the people that start watching need to be smoothly transitioned from that viral clip into what you're actually talking about. So if you care about conversion, these hooks work the best in commoditized consumer product categories, where more eyeballs almost always equals more sales. For example, this clothing brand, Wants and Needs, is doing over $100,000 per month in profit just using these clip stitch viral hooks. But on the other hand, if you're a coach, consultant, anything in info or some services businesses where you need a specific type of viewer and not just more viewers, then this probably isn't the best visual hook play. Because in this case, you're borrowing attention from those viral hooks, but then that attention won't accrue to trust for you. For most businesses, you're better off learning how to make hooks that actually attract the exact right viewer from scratch. If you want to learn how to do that and all the other visual hook types you have access to, I made this database with the top 47 visual hooks and a ton of examples for each one. Comment hooks and I'll send you the whole thing.",
